WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.540
Dutch car sharing firm My Wheels is testing Tesla FSD in its fleet to cut damaged claims and explore future autonomous rentals. This is Tesla Shorts Time, episode 588. The rollout starts with one model 3 in Den Bosch. It will scale to 50 vehicles this year under hands-free approval from the Dutch vehicle authority. My Wheels will track whether the system reduces damaged claims while customers keep a license and stay ready to intervene.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:41.460
The company also plans app-based driverless rentals in the future. Its longer-term goal is continuous vehicle circulation that cuts urban parking needs and could replace limited bus routes in some areas.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:56.420
CEO Laurenz van de Weiver expects full door-to-door autonomy within five years. While European operators test supervised autonomy on the ground, regulators in several large markets are still holding back broader rollout.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:11.100
Germany, the UK, and France have not yet granted approvals despite vehicle capability. The delays limit access for a significant portion of European owners. Tesla continues working through country-specific requirements for the software.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:24.260
Without clearances, drivers in those nations cannot use the feature even when their cars support it. As Europe sorts approvals, Tesla is already preparing the next software step for another major right-hand drive region.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:35.980
Tesla has confirmed development of FSDV14 light for Australia and New Zealand. The localized work adapts the system for right-hand drive markets and local road rules.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:44.680
It follows earlier supervised releases in other regions. Owners there have waited for builds that account for driving orientation and signage.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.400
The effort supports wider supervised FSD expansion beyond left-hand drive countries.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:02:01.340
Software progress continues even as hardware efficiency tweaks appear in current models. The Model 3 rear-wheel drive version now shows a 7% range gain in Europe.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:12.840
The improvement comes solely from new lower rolling resistance tires. No battery or software changes were involved. Updated figures now appear in European configurators for new orders.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:23.000
Buyers receive a modest efficiency lift without other specification alterations. Small product adjustments help in Europe while Tesla pushes harder on commercial vehicles.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:33.100
The largest Tesla semi-customer shared fresh details on when scaled production is expected. The timeline matters for proving long-haul economics versus diesel.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:44.560
Real-world range and total cost of ownership data will help determine how quickly more fleets sign on. Tesla has positioned the semi as a key part of its commercial vehicle strategy.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:55.160
While the semi inches forward, Tesla is also formalizing its used vehicle business. Tesla launched its official certified pre-owned program in the United States.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:04.680
The factory-backed offering includes inspection standards and coverage for the first time. It provides a structured path for buyers entering through pre-owned vehicles.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:14.160
Previously, used Tesla purchases lacked manufacturer certification. Coverage terms and buyer requirements are now outlined for participating cars.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:27.220
As Tesla refines its vehicle offerings, it is also trimming non-core services. Tesla ended its in-house vinyl wrap service after three years. Owners are now directed to third-party providers for wraps.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:40.420
The program had run through service centers since 2023. The decision aligns with focus on core vehicle and software priorities. No more official factory-supported wrap options will be available going forward.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:47.500
Resource choices inside Tesla echo the broader manufacturing lessons discussed in this week's first principal segment.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:58.480
One owner posted a full clip showing why intervention was needed during an exit lane change. The car ahead braked suddenly with a truck alongside, forcing a late manual maneuver.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:03.320
The driver noted the system could have acted earlier based on visible congestion ahead.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:16.040
Another clip captured FSD deciding to take a missed exit anyway after the driver verbally accepted continuing. The moment shows how the software sometimes overrides owner intentions in real time.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:23.540
A separate owner is preparing the first supervised FSD demonstration after a local Facebook post drew interest.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:32.100
The upcoming session will show the system to new users in person. Now, one thing worth watching is the regulatory picture in Europe.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:45.240
Tesla still faces approval barriers for supervised FSD in Germany, the UK and France. Without clearances, the software cannot be offered to drivers in those countries even as it expands elsewhere.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:51.080
The company continues to work through local requirements while competitors advance their own systems.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:57.700
Vehicle interiors have long been treated as styling exercises first and functional systems second.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:09.300
Tesla's decision to retire the white model Y option in Europe while introducing Zen Grey shows how color and material choices directly affect perceived quality and resale value.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:22.940
A single interior refresh can shift buyer preference without touching range, performance or price. European configurator updates now list the new shade on premium trims and remove the prior black and white choice.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:29.380
Owners evaluating resale will notice how these material decisions influence long term ownership costs.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.940
The same logic applies to software features that owners request for years.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:40.820
Rivians edition of Waze Alerts highlights a gap with Tesla's navigation remains closed to third-party data sources.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:49.620
When a competitor solves a long-standing pain point, the cost is measured in lost convenience and repeated owner requests rather than lost revenue.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:06:03.200
Manufacturing decisions carry similar weight. Lexus adopting gigacasting for its next EV demonstrates how a process once viewed as Tesla specific is now spreading because it cuts part count and assembly steps.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:10.220
The real test for any automaker is whether the cost savings reach the customer or stay inside the factory margin.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:20.740
Before we go, watch for more details on how the semi-production timeline shapes up against diesel economics in the coming weeks, and that's a wrap on today's Tesla news.

